      <section class="hero shell">
        <div class="eyebrow">Embodied HRI &amp; Intelligent-Cockpit Interaction · Independent Design Research · 2022</div>
        <h1>Proactive Embodied Voice Assistant for Safety-Critical Driving</h1>
        <p class="lede">Exploring how speech, text, affective expression, and escalating alerts can work as one coherent social signal when an in-vehicle assistant needs to interrupt the driver.</p>
        <div class="chips"><span>Independent project</span><span>6 exploratory interviews</span><span>Proactive HRI</span><span>Multimodal feedback</span></div>
        <figure class="hero-figure"><img :src="images.overview" alt="Portfolio overview showing an expressive in-vehicle voice assistant across cockpit states" /><figcaption>The project reframed an in-vehicle voice assistant as an embodied interaction partner rather than a voice-only command interface.</figcaption></figure>
      </section>

      <section class="shell two-column">
        <div><p class="kicker">Design question</p><h2>How should an embodied assistant communicate urgency without becoming distracting or socially abrasive?</h2></div>
        <div class="prose"><p>Most in-vehicle assistants wait for a command. Safety-critical driving creates a different interaction problem: the system may need to initiate contact, escalate a warning, and make the required action legible within seconds.</p><p>I explored whether a consistent character, progressive affect, and coordinated verbal and visual cues could make these interventions easier to interpret while preserving a sense of companionship.</p></div>
      </section>

      <section class="metrics-band"><div class="shell metric-grid"><div><strong>6</strong><span>exploratory semi-structured interviews</span></div><div><strong>4</strong><span>voice-assistant references compared</span></div><div><strong>3 levels</strong><span>progressive states for drowsiness and distraction</span></div><div><strong>4 modes</strong><span>speech, text, expression, and contextual display</span></div></div></section>

      <section class="shell process">
        <div><p class="kicker">Exploratory process</p><h2>From social expectations to a coherent interaction character</h2></div>
        <ol class="steps"><li><span>01</span><h3>Review precedents</h3><p>Compared the embodiment, personality, tone, and functional behavior of four automotive voice assistants.</p></li><li><span>02</span><h3>Interview drivers</h3><p>Used six exploratory interviews to identify desired qualities, disliked behaviors, and expectations for proactive support.</p></li><li><span>03</span><h3>Frame the character</h3><p>Organized identity, temperament, voice, and interaction strategy into a practical design framework.</p></li><li><span>04</span><h3>Prototype escalation</h3><p>Mapped safety contexts to coordinated language, affective states, and in-cockpit visual feedback.</p></li></ol>
      </section>

      <section class="artifact artifact--light"><div class="shell artifact__inner"><div><p class="kicker">Scenario framework</p><h2>Proactive interventions were structured by context and urgency</h2><p class="prose">The design covered repeated drowsiness and distraction alerts, dangerous behavior, takeover support, traffic-light departure, and child-lock status. The emphasis was not on adding a face to every message, but on defining when the assistant should intervene and how its behavior should intensify.</p></div><figure><img :src="images.scenarios" alt="Scenario framework mapping proactive driving situations to speech, text, and expressive sketches" /><figcaption>A scenario-to-feedback matrix connected system context with verbal and embodied responses.</figcaption></figure></div></section>

      <section class="shell design-system"><div><p class="kicker">Embodied feedback</p><h2>A small visual vocabulary made system intent glanceable</h2><p class="prose">Expressions encoded sleepiness, concern, urgency, takeover, distraction, movement, and child-lock states. Repeated alerts progressed from gentle awareness to explicit urgency instead of repeating the same notification.</p></div><figure><img :src="images.expressions" alt="Expression-state design for drowsiness, distraction, takeover, stop-and-go, and child-lock scenarios" /><figcaption>The concept used expression intensity and contextual symbols as redundant cues alongside speech and text.</figcaption></figure></section>

      <section class="prototype"><div class="shell prototype__inner"><div><p class="kicker">Cockpit integration</p><h2>One character, multiple levels of interruption</h2><p class="prose">The final concept placed the assistant inside the central driving interface and allowed its scale, affect, text, and placement to change with the urgency of the situation. These images document a visual interaction prototype, not an implemented driver-monitoring algorithm.</p></div><figure><img :src="images.cockpit" alt="Cockpit interface concepts showing the voice assistant responding to several driving situations" /></figure></div></section>

      <section class="shell opportunity"><div><p class="kicker">Research opportunity</p><h2>A foundation for a focused HRI study</h2></div><div class="opportunity-list"><p><span>Embodiment</span>Compare voice-only feedback with voice plus an expressive visual agent.</p><p><span>Escalation</span>Test whether adaptive expressions improve urgency comprehension without increasing annoyance.</p><p><span>Calibration</span>Measure whether the assistant helps drivers distinguish advisory, warning, and takeover-critical states.</p><p><span>Behavior</span>Evaluate response accuracy, response time, perceived urgency, trust calibration, and interruption cost.</p></div></section>

      <section class="disclosure"><div class="shell disclosure__inner"><strong>Evidence boundary</strong><p>This page documents an independent exploratory design project completed in 2022. It does not claim implementation of a DMS algorithm, a controlled trust or workload experiment, or statistically generalizable findings. Percentages from the six interviews are intentionally not presented as population-level evidence.</p></div></section>
